//
// 1. RECONSIDERATION OF EXCLUDED TEMPLATE ELEMENTS:
//
// - primary_care_visit_capacity (Care Providers):
//   DECISION: Remain Excluded.
//   STRUCTURAL REASONING: Our map explicitly isolates 'primary_care_clinician_supply' and 'specialty_care_referral_availability'. Including generic visit capacity creates redundant parallel paths without adding distinct systemic feedback regarding gestational legal deadlines [ACOG 2023].
//   CITATION: American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2023), "Advanced Practice Clinicians and Abortion Care Provision."
//
// - uninsured_population_size (Financing / Insurance):
//   DECISION: Reconsider for Future Inclusion (Currently Excluded).
//   STRUCTURAL REASONING (Sienna revision): Uninsured status and Medicaid enrollment are distinct. Abortion coverage depends on state policy and exceptions, and other assistance may pay costs. Keep excluded for now because R2 focuses on financing difficulty; revisit to compare coverage groups [3, 9].
//   CITATION: KFF (2024), "The Hyde Amendment and Coverage for Abortion Services Under Medicaid in the Post-Roe Era."
//
// - housing_stability (Structural Determinants):
//   DECISION: Remain Excluded.
//   STRUCTURAL REASONING: Travel and lodging costs resulting from local care bans are modeled directly via 'out_of_state_travel_burden', while household liquid capital is captured by 'income_security'. Adding housing stability duplicates these mechanisms without creating a distinct feedback loop [ANSIRH 2024].
//   CITATION: ANSIRH (2024), "Abortion seekers, particularly those who must travel out of state are at higher risk of incurring catastrophic health expenditures."
//
// - trust_in_health_institutions (Public Health):
//   DECISION: Reconsider for Future Inclusion (Currently Excluded).
//   STRUCTURAL REASONING: Trust moderates whether patients act on public health messaging. However, 'health_communication_effectiveness' currently serves as the primary operational proxy in Loop R3 to prevent over-complicating the public health feedback flow [WHO 2025].
//
//   CITATION: World Health Organization (2025), "Abortion care guideline, 2nd ed."
// - occupational_stratification_by_race_gender (Structural Inequities)
//   DECISION: Retain as contextual element, remove from owned closed loop.
//   STRUCTURAL REASONING: Employment patterns may shape income, but the current
//sources do not establish reproductive-care spending changing occupational segregation. Its income connection remains explicitly hypothetical.
//
// - medicaid_enrollment (Financing / Insurance) 
//   DECISION: Retain, specify pregnant women ages 14-44, condition spending link.
//   STRUCTURAL REASONING: Enrollment and service coverage are different; coverage must be usable for patient spending to decrease [3, 9].
//
// - household_out_of_pocket_spending (Funding)
//   DECISION: Retain and specify reproductive care, including abortion.
//   STRUCTURAL REASONING: Payments can strain household resources [9, 10], but actual spending does not measure unmet need. Income security is not redefined as financial strain.
//
// 2. PROPOSAL OF NEW ELEMENTS (NOT IN ORIGINAL TEMPLATE):
//
// - Proposed Element 1: Out-of-state travel burden
//   AREA: Structural Determinants / Geographic Access
//   STRUCTURAL JUSTIFICATION: Converts state policy bans into indirect household expenses (lodging, mileage, childcare, lost wages), creating a crucial structural link between state restrictions and household out-of-pocket financial strain [Guttmacher 2023].
//   CITATION: Guttmacher Institute (2023), "The High Toll of US Abortion Bans: Nearly One in Five Patients Now Traveling Out of State for Abortion Care."
//
// - Proposed Element 2: Abortion fund capacity
//   AREA: Funding / Practical Support
//   STRUCTURAL JUSTIFICATION: Represents the capacity of nonprofit abortion funds to provide financial and practical assistance. Grants can reduce household out-of-pocket spending when Medicaid does not cover abortion or patients cannot use that coverage [ANSIRH 2024; Guttmacher 2023].
//   CITATION: ANSIRH (2024); Guttmacher Institute (2023).

// - Proposed Element 3: Policy advocacy
//   AREA: Public Health
//   STRUCTURAL JUSTIFICATION: Limited access to reproductive care may increase pressure for advocacy. Patients and organizations can then push for changes to scope-of-practice laws that improve access. This provides the proposed mechanism that closes B1, and policy change may take time.
//
// - Proposed Element 4: Difficulty raising funds for abortion care
//   AREA: Financing / Access.
//   STRUCTURAL JUSTIFICATION: Separates financing gaps from payments. [9] describes raising funds through delayed expenses; [11] associates financial barriers with later presentation; [13] explains the mechanism.
//   CITATION: Jones (2024) [9]; Mackinnon Krems et al. (2024) [11]; Weitz (2024) [13].
//
// - Proposed Element 5: Delay in obtaining abortion care
//   AREA: Care Providers / Access.
//   STRUCTURAL JUSTIFICATION: Financial/geographic obstacles are associated with later presentation [11]. Chasing the fee [13] supports a proposed return to higher costs, conditional on coverage and actual receipt of care.
//   CITATION: Mackinnon Krems et al. (2024) [11]; Weitz (2024) [13].
// SIENNA R2 RESEARCH REVISION: Older owned sources replaced with 2024 publications.
  // Retain shared elements and teammates' definitions. Two negative arrows make R2 reinforcing.
  // [9], [10], [11] are observational research; [13] is commentary; [3] is policy context.
  // Publication after Dobbs does not imply post-Dobbs data: [9] uses June 2021-July 2022;
  // [10] uses January-June 2019; [11] uses October 2019-March 2020.
  // These studies inform mechanisms, not effects caused by Dobbs or the complete loop.
  // Studies do not uniformly isolate pregnant women ages 14-44.
  // Income security means stable resources/earnings in the shared map; evidence about
  // basic-needs strain does not prove a lasting earnings loss. Keep return conditional.
  // Required fees and actual payments differ; aid, coverage, method, and nonreceipt matter.
